{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The paper asks how partisan polarization in online COVID-19 discourse varied across subnational regions and over time in the United States and Canada. To answer this, it develops a new computational way to measure that variation.", "quotes": [{"text": "This study introduces a novel computational approach to measure subnational and event-driven variation in partisan polarization and explores these dynamics across the United States and Canada.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "It asks specifically how regional polarization correlates with three things: a region's ideological leaning, exposure to conspiracy-related content, and vaccination rates.", "quotes": [{"text": "Specifically, we analyze millions of tweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions; (2) exposure to conspiracy theory-related content; and (3) vaccination rates.", "page": 3}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "Conservative regions showed higher partisan polarization in both countries, and the effect was stronger in the United States.", "quotes": [{"text": "Our analysis reveals that politically conservative regions exhibited significantly higher levels of partisan polarization in both countries, with this effect particularly pronounced in the United States.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "Polarization is not uniform. It varies by geography, political leaning and health topic, and the two countries differ substantially.", "quotes": [{"text": "we have demonstrated that polarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial differences between the two countries.", "page": 25}]}, {"claim": "In the U.S., polarization in vaccine discourse is strongly and negatively linked to vaccination rates, which the authors read as a sign of real public health consequences. This link is much weaker in Canada.", "quotes": [{"text": "We demonstrate a strong negative correlation between regional vaccination rates and the degree of polarization in vaccine-related discussions in the U.S., suggesting tangible public health implications of online partisan division.", "page": 1}, {"text": "This relationship was notably weaker in Canada, pointing to important cross-national differences in how political polarization manifests and impacts health behaviors.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "Polarization rises in short spikes tied to specific political events and policy announcements, and these spikes last only a few days.", "quotes": [{"text": "These polarization surges typically lasted only a few days, revealing the dynamic nature of online partisan discourse.", "page": 1}]}], "evidence": [{"claim": "The data are a 1% real-time sample of tweets from the official Twitter/X API, covering the United States and Canada from October 2020 to January 2021. The sample was filtered with political keywords.", "quotes": [{"text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and Canada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "After filtering for COVID-19 content, the dataset contains about 12.5 million U.S. tweets and 46.6 million Canadian tweets.", "quotes": [{"text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from 1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.", "page": 5}]}, {"claim": "The study also draws on external data: COVID-19 case and death counts, vaccination rates, and U.S. voter registration records.", "quotes": [{"text": "We collected Twitter (X) data, COVID-19 case data from official government websites, vaccination rates from public health records, and U.S. voter registration records from state government portals.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Tweets were embedded with domain-adapted RoBERTa models. Polarization was measured as the separation of left- and right-leaning users in embedding space, using t