icant, partly due to the smaller number of regions analyzed.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "Across U.S. states, polarization in vaccine discourse is strongly and negatively correlated with later vaccination rates. No similar pattern appears in Canada.", "quotes": [{"text": "we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between vaccine polarization and vaccination rates.", "page": 16}, {"text": "We did not observe a similar pattern in Canada, due to small sample size and the implementation of vaccine mandates.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "Daily polarization peaks often coincide with contentious events specific to each country, and they follow a quick, symmetric rise and fall lasting days.", "quotes": [{"text": "we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with highly contentious events specific to each country’s context.", "page": 17}, {"text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig 14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.", "page": 19}]}, {"claim": "Conservative partisans in both countries post conspiracy-related content more often than progressive partisans. Conspiracy volume is weakly but significantly negatively correlated with daily polarization in the U.S., with no significant correlation in Canada.", "quotes": [{"text": "show that conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive partisans.", "page": 21}, {"text": "When correlated with aggregate daily polarization (Fig 15 B and D, we find a small but significant negative correlation for the United States, while no significant correlation was observed for Canada.", "page": 21}]}, {"claim": "Canada shows similar patterns to the U.S. but much lower overall polarization, and its association between conservative leaning and polarization is weaker.", "quotes": [{"text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.", "page": 25}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "Semantic similarity is only a proxy for polarization and may miss signals that embeddings do not capture.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic embedding representations.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Because tweets are aggregated into one embedding per user, nuances such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be lost.", "quotes": [{"text": "As a result, subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent, while consistent linguistic cues, including negation, are preserved in the aggregated representation.", "page": 10}]}, {"claim": "Party labels rely on self-reported information, and Bloc Québécois supporters were excluded.", "quotes": [{"text": "we encountered few users who identified as Bloc Québécois supporters and excluded them from analysis.", "page": 24}, {"text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies on self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Twitter users are more engaged and more partisan than the general public, so the results reflect only a vocal subset of discourse.", "quotes": [{"text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Only English-language tweets were analyzed, which may bias the Canadian results, including the high polarization found for Quebec.", "quotes": [{"text": "Fourth, we restricted our analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias.", "page": 24}]}, {"claim": "Self-reported locations, VPNs and bots may introduce error, and bots may inflate the apparent partisan distance.", "quotes": [{"text": "Automated accounts can contribute disproportionately to partisan discourse by generating large volumes of ideologically slanted content or amplifying existing narratives, potentially inflating the apparent semantic distance between political groups.", "page": 25}]}, {"claim": "The U.S. and Canadian datasets were built with different keyword strategies, which may partly account for the cross-national differences.", "quotes": [{"text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization, and this should be considered when interpreting the comparative results.", "page": 25}]}, {"claim": "The tweets come from late 2020 while the vaccination data come from 2021, so the vaccine correlations are suggestive rather than causal. More generally, the paper's correlations do not establish causation.", "quotes": [{"text": "As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions and later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.", "page": 25}, {"text": "Finally, while several of our analyses rely on correlations, these results do not imply causation.", "page": 25}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors extend prior research on partisan COVID-19 responses. They agree with its finding that right-leaning regions were more divided, then go further by characterizing geographic heterogeneity and change over time.", "quotes": [{"text": "However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal evolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.", "page": 22}]}, {"claim": "They place themselves among computational, embedding-based text approaches to polarization. They acknowledge network-based ideology inference but choose a strictly text-based method because access to network data has become restricted.", "quotes": [{"t